\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12\\cdot \\cancel{11!}}{\\cancel{11!}} \\\\  & =15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =32\\,760.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080614 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080618 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080622 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080627 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390295 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390300 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390304 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390309 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1071 "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186083599 1071 "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_{n + 1} = 2a_{n}, a_{1} = 3, n ∈ N$.\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$a_{n} = 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n = 1$:$3(2)$ {{field-1}} **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n =$ {{field-2}}, we have\n$2^{k} - 1$ {{field-3}} **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n$3^{k} - 1$ {{field-4}} **Conclusion**: Therefore, $a_{n} = 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}$ for all $n ∈ N$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186083599 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1):\\,{a}_{1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{1-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=3\\cdot {2}^{k-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{(k+1)-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389229 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389242 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389249 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388687 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389290 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390631 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390520 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390527 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390454 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186087835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390459 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186088136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085414 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186088136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390625 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186088136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186088136 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=3.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186088136 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$a(t) = ⟨8t^{3},-1⟩$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨ - 1, 3⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t = 3$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186088136 1074 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{4}-3,\\,-t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)^{4}-3,\\,-(3)+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨159,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390700 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390717 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390724 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092231 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389022 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389041 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389048 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389055 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388840 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186096653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388875 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186096659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390564 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390373 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186096678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390380 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186096680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390387 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186100872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388416 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186100874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388409 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186100928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389723 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098157 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186101142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388423 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101142 1071 "Find $Ax$ if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186101142 1071 "Find $Ax$ if \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101142 1074 "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-6)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$Ax=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098187 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186101147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388511 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101147 1071 "Calculate the product $I_{2}B$ given that $I_{2}$ is the $2 \\cdot 2$ identity matrix and\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186101147 1071 "Calculate the product ${I}_{2}B$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186101147 1074 "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}\\cdot B=B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390394 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390401 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389573 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388938 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388889 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388896 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388903 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390408 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186105902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390440 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186106107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102488 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186106107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390353 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186106107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186106107 1071 "Let $a = ⟨ - 2, 8, 3⟩$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186106107 1071 "Let $a=⟨-2,8,3⟩.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186106107 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}\\cdot ⟨-2,8,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186110589 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388777 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186110590 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388770 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186110674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390346 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390591 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390651 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390658 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390665 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389587 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390672 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390679 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390847 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390743 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390804 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186112105 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389339 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115521 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t) = 2i + t^{2}j + \\ln (t + 1)k$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115521 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=2\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,k.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115521 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $2$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/44/44e047a975ee3b2ad7966b95bbba3a6270925f451c8cb8c6dac6dbef532f2b7a.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113214 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390585 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115713 1071 "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x = 2$ for $-360^{∘} \\le x < 0^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115713 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115713 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={60}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113496 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390731 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115760 1071 "Find the limit  $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)$, if $f(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{t-4}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115760 1071 "Find the limit $lim_(t → 2)f(t)$, if $f(t) = t^{2}i + \\frac{t - 4}{2}j + \\frac{1}{t}k$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115760 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t})\\,k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2} & ={2}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2} & =\\frac{2-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=4\\,i-j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113556 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390750 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115770 1071 "Find $(f \\cdot g)^{′}(t)$ where\n$f(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3t^{2} \\\\ t + 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, g(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}t + 2 \\\\ -2t^{2} \\\\ 5t\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186115770 1071 "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ where\n\n$$\nf(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} \\\\ t+4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+2 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186115770 1074 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6t \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4t \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6t & 1 & 0 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}6t & 0 \\\\ t+2 & 5t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}6t & 1 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & t+4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4t & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}t+4 & 8 \\\\ -4t & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & 8 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2} & t+4 \\\\ 1 & -4t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390913 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186119787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390797 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186119794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388638 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186119805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390763 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186119835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390854 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117469 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186120223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390835 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120223 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(2,-5, 3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n = ⟨ - 1, 2,-3⟩$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186120223 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(2,-5,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120223 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-5,3⟩,$ and $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =⟨2,-5,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117475 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186120224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390841 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120224 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(3, 1, 5), B(6,-8, 0)$, and $C(-2, 1, 3)$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186120224 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(3,1,5),\\,B(6,-8,0)$, and $C(-2,1,3).$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120224 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨6,-8,0⟩-⟨3,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-9,-5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨-2,1,3⟩-⟨3,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $a=⟨3,1,5⟩$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s\\overset{\\to }{AB}+t\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨3,-9,-5⟩+t⟨-5,0,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117517 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186120231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390881 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186120231 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(5,-3, 1)$ and is parallel to the lines $r = ⟨8, 1, 0⟩ + s⟨3,