 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390512 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389496 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129296 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389503 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389489 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389607 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389614 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389621 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390783 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390790 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388605 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388598 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126428 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388586 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129493 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = \\cos 2t, y = \\cos t, t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{4}]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129493 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129493 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t(16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126452 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388592 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129497 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 2\\sin t, y = 2\\cos t, t ∈ [0, π]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129497 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129497 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126560 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389035 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129515 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - v \\\\ 4u - v^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$,\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129515 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129515 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ 4u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & >0 \\\\ -4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126673 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389270 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129534 1071 "![](images/c4/c420d45ca9418fd29889d91a49cce4ae80bb71bdfd4e4aa701b495cc8d87335f.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x, y, z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2} + y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z$, then $x$, and then $y$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129534 1071 "![](images/c4/c420d45ca9418fd29889d91a49cce4ae80bb71bdfd4e4aa701b495cc8d87335f.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129534 1074 "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b7cfa72ed3dbfb121039c6e64b843d83489878a9f457fb1386e455e7d9649ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f49af5da2e4f1f281700e16c4659fabfcd0b15661947ca985d38f208f8563877.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126797 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389415 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129555 1071 "A circular plate of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $2\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $4\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129555 1071 "A circular plate of mass $2kg$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $2m$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $4m$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129555 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{2}{4\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $4\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+32 \\\\  & =36\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126815 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389483 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129558 1071 "![](images/f0/f0ca128a0755c0419d03d119501210b4be8036ccc0148f21b0e3a3665def30d5.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $10\\,m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $3\\,kg$ and is $4\\,m$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129558 1071 "![](images/f0/f0ca128a0755c0419d03d119501210b4be8036ccc0148f21b0e3a3665def30d5.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $10m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $3kg$ and is $4m$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129558 1074 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $10\\,m$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,m.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n10-4=6\\,m,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a68789963345d21ca403d22a5f97ee440b0c3baa36345b0161bc7f80a8e0d1e0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m\\cdot 6=-6m\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $3\\cdot 4=12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-6m & =0 \\\\ 6m & =12 \\\\ m & =2\\,kg.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126907 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126910 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126914 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390470 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390474 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390477 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129576 1071 "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186129576 1071 "Classify the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = 2a_{n - 1} - 3n^{2}, a_{1} = 10, n \\ge 2$.\nThe recurrence relation $a_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186129576 1074 "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, the relation has order $1.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-1}$ appears in the first power with constant  coefficient $2.$ So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-3{n}^{2}$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$1,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388645 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388715 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388729 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388966 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388973 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388980 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388722 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389008 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388798 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388805 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388631 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388959 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186133848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388680 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186134137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130948 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186134137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389236 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186134137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186134137 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z = 10 - x^{2} - y^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x + y = 1$, $x = 0$, and $y = 0$. Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $curlF = ⟨ - 2z, 0, 0⟩$, the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr =$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186134137 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x+y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186134137 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389955 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389968 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389981 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390433 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390211 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152094 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390218 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186152165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388910 13194139533463 1759218