86157615 1071 "![](images/5f/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n$Δ = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 1, 0 \\le v \\le 1}$\n\nand the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\nwhere\n\n$x = v, y = u(1 + v^{2})$.\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T$.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ$.*" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157615 1074 "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](images/46/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153974 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389690 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157628 1071 "> The product of any two integers is divisible by those two integers.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157628 1071 "The product of any two integers is divisible by those two integers.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157628 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"any\" indicates that we use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to two integers. So, by denoting our numbers as $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n∀\\,a,b\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(a∣ab)\\,∧\\,(b∣ab)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows:\n\n$∀a,b\\in Z,\\,(a∣ab)\\,∧\\,(b∣ab)$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153980 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389696 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157629 1071 "> The square of every real number is non-negative.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157629 1071 "The square of every real number is non-negative.\n\nExpress the statement above in formal language." 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157629 1074 "Let's examine our statement:\n\n- The word \"every\" indicates we should use a universal quantifier, $∀.$\n\n- The sentence refers to real numbers. So, by denoting a real number as $x,$ we have that \n\n$$\n∀\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\n- Finally, the condition in the statement can be written as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we write the statement as follows: \n\n$∀\\,x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154028 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154031 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154034 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154037 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389943 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389946 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389949 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389952 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $A$ | $B$ | $¬((A∧B)∧A)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $T$ | $T$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $T$ | $F$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $F$ | $T$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $F$ | $F$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157637 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬((A∧B)∧A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157637 1074 "First, we add a column for the part of the expression that's in parentheses, $A∧B:$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nThen, we add a column for $(A∧B)∧A:$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧B)∧A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nFinally, we add a column for the negation $¬((A∧B)∧A):$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A∧B\\,$ | $\\,(A∧B)∧A\\,$ | $\\,¬((A∧B)∧A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154680 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390464 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157699 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $B=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{ln}⁡B.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157699 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $B = PDP^{-1}$, find $\\ln B$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157699 1074 "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the given matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡B=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡B & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155296 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390811 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157748 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157748 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$*" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186157748 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $λ_{1} = \\textrm{i}$.*" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186157748 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186161994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388791 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162023 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388784 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388763 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162047 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388819 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388544 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162095 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388551 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162096 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388537 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388558 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388565 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388572 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388517 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389015 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162104 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388624 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159278 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186162351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162351 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162351 1071 "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186162351 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159290 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186162353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388505 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162353 1071 "For the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, find the pseudoinverse $A^{+}$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186162353 1071 "For the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186162353 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388812 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391056 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166915 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390977 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166915 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390984 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166915 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390991 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166915 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390998 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166916 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391005 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166916 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391016 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166916 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391027 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166916 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391038 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391102 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391109 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391116 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391123 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166917 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391130 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166918 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391049 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391063 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390945 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390961 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391070 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391086 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166976 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390861 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186166976 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390887 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163918 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389594 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167145 1071 "If\n$u(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$v(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ -\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$\nare solutions to $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t - \\cos t \\\\ \\cos t + \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167145 1071 "If\n$u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]$\nare solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167145 1074 "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nw(t)=u(t)-v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164193 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164198 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390903 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390908 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167201 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167201 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167201 1074 "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x+3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/6b/6ba5066fd4ddc43491c3d007355e0b64c84d449d6462812aa4c8ca0450887a8b.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164209 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390920 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167203 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167203 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167203 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164433 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186167234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391137 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186167234 1074 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (straight to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/0d/0d010be8bad72bf4ebac4dbe2d325f1a1f194d78c676e2a62306b5c7d2af3c66.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going to the right  but then starts turning downward.\n\n![](images/51/5118424696b9602f10bf116c3a292e2fb58ed2f9acd2a9507888ab92a9b8986b.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues to move rightward and bend downward, converging to the origin.\n\n![](images/92/92a58573efafd7155cf02faf52e27ab968d9161ad448530b45673fd83a61fde1.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389276 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389283 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389297 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389304 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172139 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389132 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389373 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172140 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389380 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169149 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186172386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389139 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172386 1071 "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y = x^{4}$ is rotated by $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x ∈ [- 2, 2]$." 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186172386 1071 "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={x}^{4}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186172386 1074 "Notice that $y={x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=4{x}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175976 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389545 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175976 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389559 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175977 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389566 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175978 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389524 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175978 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389531 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389517 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186175979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389538 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186176002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389166 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186176002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389173 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186176074 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389552 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186179707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390770 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186179708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388924 131941