 N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7a/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186198848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196082 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186198848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196088 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186198848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196094 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186198848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390166 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186198848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390172 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186198848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390177 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186198848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186198848 1071 "Describe the set $S = {5, 6, 7,..., 50}$ using a conditional definition.\n\n$N$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186198848 1071 "Describe the set $S={5,6,7,...,50}$ using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS={x\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}}}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186198848 1074 "The set $S$ contains all natural numbers between $5$ and $50$ (including $5$ and $50$).\n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS={x\\in N\\,:\\,5\\le x\\le 50}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example:\n\n- We can write $x\\in Z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nS={x\\in Z\\,:\\,5\\le x\\le 50}.\n$$\n\n- We can write $4<x<51.$ This gives\n\n$$\nS={x\\in N\\,:\\,4<x<51},\n$$\n\nor, writing $x\\in Z$ as before, we have\n\n$$\nS={x\\in Z\\,:\\,4<x<51}.\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389580 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390618 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186217159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390571 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186217159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390686 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186217159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390693 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221442 1038 #edb/ref 17592186390926 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220194 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220199 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220204 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220208 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390817 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390822 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390826 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390831 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Suppose we wish to find $L{e^{2t}f(t)}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$e^{-st} \\cdot e^{2t}f(t)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}" 13194139533463 17592186045585 false] [17592186221937 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{2t}f(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below. By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186221937 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}f(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186229160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186391143 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388396 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388395 1069 "q-135540" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388395 1071 "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-7+2\\text{i} \\\\ -2-7\\text{i} \\\\ -2-2\\text{i} \\\\ -3-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5-5\\text{i} \\\\ -7 \\\\ -2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2+\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388395 1072 #uuid "665587c5-1a3a-55cd-89d1-5eea160afc62" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388395 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388397 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388398 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388399 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388400 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388401 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1079 #edb/ref 17592186388400 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388396 1080 #uuid "a66467bc-9a50-5d8e-837a-eeefeb49dc48" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388397 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388397 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388397 1084 #uuid "81edab10-6956-528b-91cb-f9fb1e627150" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388398 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -7 & 0 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388398 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388398 1084 #uuid "a2a94d76-71ed-5a8e-981f-0131b6dc27f3" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388399 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388399 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388399 1084 #uuid "7a2c8fa7-7d76-5149-a9f4-9a3fa3c87f25" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true