 $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $16$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388624 1072 #uuid "82b609f1-ebbe-5986-a6ff-a556a13681d3" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388624 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={8}^{2}=64,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={4}^{2}=16.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=16$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388626 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388627 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388628 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388629 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388630 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1079 #edb/ref 17592186388627 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388625 1080 #uuid "e12f6b66-e0b5-5c3d-87c1-2792f81acfc8" 1