 matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388814 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388815 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388816 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388817 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388818 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388813 1079 #edb/ref 175921863888