 teenagers would eventually go to school on electric scooters if the listed probabilities remained constant over many years?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388826 1072 #uuid "a031dbeb-978c-5542-b4e9-e4e60f2dc984" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388826 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.8 & 0.2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.6 & 0.4 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.4 & -0.4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=5{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=5{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3})-{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{4}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the teenagers will go to school in electric scooters. Therefore, the total number of teenagers in the city that will go to school in electric scooters will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\,400=1\\,700.\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388828 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388829 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388830 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388831 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388832 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1079 #edb/ref 17592186388832 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186388827 1080 #uu