[17592186389083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389084 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389083 1069 "q-160909" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389083 1071 "![](images/95/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389083 1072 #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389083 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389085 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389086 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389087 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389088 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389089 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389084 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 175921860