 }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389092 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389093 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389094 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389095 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389096 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1079 #edb/ref 17592186389094 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389091 1080 #uuid "6dd89d67-b436-5e5f