d631b01f66a9fd547f475.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389125 1072 #uuid "a368d06f-dd38-5739-a1e1-1669cc437d2c" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389125 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389127 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389128 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389129 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389130 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389131 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1079 #edb/ref 17592186389127 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389126 1080 #uuid "2c650825-40f2-5963-96ba-a65375cf218b" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389127 1081 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389127 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389127 1084 #uuid "00aa38ea-bac0-52a0-86dc-bc6b9414c3ba" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389128 1081 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389128 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389128 1084 #uuid "d3398719-8fe7-5303-a753-c43f72bb0596" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389129 1081 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389129 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389129 1084 #uuid "b175c8ee-c27d-5255-8737-063a160f1607" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389130 1081 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389130 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389130 1084 #uuid "1a083a94-f096-531d-ba65-44631b40cc71" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389131 1081 "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389131 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389131 1084 #uuid "4ca6e142-ca44-5238-9857-d72f2dd5f577" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389133 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389132 1069 "q-161091" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389132 1071 "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x={y}^{3}+1$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,1].$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389132 1072 #uuid "0abbafda-b663-58bc-a5e8-afa7f578ba96" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389132 1074 "Notice that $x={y}^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389134 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389135 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389136 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389137 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389138 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389133 1077 "selection" 13194139533463 1759