is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2(x-2),2(y-1),2(z-2)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ \\\\ ⟨x-2,y-1,z-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨x,y,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2=\\lambda x \\\\ y-1=\\lambda y \\\\ z-2=\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=9$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{4}{(1-\\lambda )^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1-\\lambda )^{2}}+\\frac{4}{(1-\\lambda )^{2}} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{9}{(1-\\lambda )^{2}} & =9 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )^{2} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\lambda  & =\\pm 1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm 1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we get the critical point $(2,1,2).$\n\n- For $\\lambda =2,$ we get the critical point $(-2,-1,-2).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-2,-1,-2)\\,$ | $\\,(2,1,2)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(2,1,2),$ and its minimum value is $0.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389360 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389361 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [1759