2186389434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389434 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(4t+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389434 1072 #uuid "e3727b71-f77c-52d3-b736-d8fbf4a3880a" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389434 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=4t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=2(0)^{2}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+t+3.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)^{2}+1+3=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)^{2}+2+3=13\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {13}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =338-72 \\\\  & =266\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 1319