 in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389469 1072 #uuid "f633b7bf-41b1-55e5-94fa-734d0d539fd4" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389469 1074 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{6}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}({1}^{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389471 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389472 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389470 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389473 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389470 