 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{8}{5}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389476 1072 #uuid "8c495a30-23c5-530b-9d91-e188ad9ee72e" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389476 1074 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2})=6\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {6}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{5}-\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389478 131