 true] [17592186389489 1069 "q-163802" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389489 1071 "![](images/97/97045288ee8cefe694fe6e30d7153d2cc78eb2c73a254f366f443f7230312148.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $10\\,m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $4\\,kg$ and is $6\\,m$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389489 1072 #uuid "bfbc2cfa-782c-593d-b6ed-7e319d5ccc9f" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389489 1074 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $10\\,m$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $6\\,m.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n10-6=4\\,m,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec39adee65bb0219053a8802470534cf8b2588f95cc8338c5c4a392adac6d516.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m\\cdot 4=-4m\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $4\\cdot 6=24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24-4m & =0 \\\\ 4m & =24 \\\\ m & =6\\,kg.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389491 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389492 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389494 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389495 13194139533463 17592186045585 true