ametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=2Z,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389566 1072 #uuid "6585630a-9b94-5ba5-b2b2-756dcac0fca6" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186389566 1074 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=2Z,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2Z,3Y,5X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\th