e equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\n So, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\n we obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n \n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n As a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n Now, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $||y||=1,$ as usual.\n\n Since we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $12,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\nmax{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1}={\\lambda }_{max}=12.\n$$\n\n Next, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=12:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-12I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]-12[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Seeking a non-zero solution of $(B-12I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\n Finally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\