rices:\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${3}^{K/2}.$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390505 1072 #uuid "01aa6efd-1aa0-5169-9c59-06c6dc6237ee" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390505 1074 "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={3}^{x/2}$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{K/2}={3}^{\\frac{1}{2}PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $4$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{3}^{J/2}=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{K/2} & =P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-9 \\\\ -18 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390506 107