 true] [17592186390605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390606 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390605 1069 "q-3121" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390605 1071 "![](images/9a/9a87ae2b230e66b685051ee7d13987f5f68690a07a67ade9e7af0eb870f6224f.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390605 1072 #uuid "c69be2ea-7b70-598d-bb9a-251384a82770" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390605 1074 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e7/e77170aac6d81b1cfd0e93804b0c59802f28089d24aeaff065da04cabc44c172.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390607 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390608 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390609 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390610 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390611 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533463 17592186045585 true] [17592186390606 1077 "selection" 13194